    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0087-optional-token-counting-capability-for-the-context-budget-guard.md",
      "@type": [
        "ADR",
        "TechArticle"
      ],
      "name": "ADR-0087: An Optional count_tokens Capability for the Context-Budget Guard",
      "identifier": "ADR-0087",
      "status": "accepted",
      "datePublished": "2026-08-01",
      "description": "Lets a backend that owns a real tokenizer measure the C2 context-budget pre-flight, while leaving the conservative estimator as the default. Context: ADR-0066 deliberately hardened _estimate_tokens into an upper bound (ASCII ~3 chars/token, non-ASCII/CJK 2 tokens/char) because under-counting was the failure that let a CJK-heavy prompt reach Ollama front-truncation or an MLX KV overrun, and no tokenizer was installable. A 2026-08-01 comparison against Apple's SystemLanguageModel.token_count measured the resulting over-count at 1.73-1.95x on this agent's own corpora (identity.md 232->134, constitution 867->453, rules 516->264, 37 skills 31,009->17,958, 10 patterns 479->263) — the ratio tracks the CJK rule directly, since the value layer is Japanese-dominant. The cost scales inversely with window size: on a 4,096-token backend the guard's skip floor (MIN_CLAMPED_NUM_PREDICT=2048) puts the effective input ceiling at ~1,140 real tokens, 28% of the window. Two independent things consume the rest and the ADR is careful not to conflate them: exact counting raises the ceiling to 4096-2048-64 = 1,984 real tokens (48%), so the estimator's over-count costs about 21 percentage points and the output-floor reservation costs the remaining ~50% — a separate variable this ADR deliberately does not move. Measuring for real roughly doubles usable input, which is less than the raw gap suggests. The measurement evidence was not preserved (the probe ran in a non-durable scratchpad, so there is no docs/evidence/adr-0087/ to link), which the ADR records as a defect in its own record rather than glossing. The built-in Ollama path cannot be fixed directly for two checked reasons: /api/tokenize and /api/detokenize both 404 on the running 0.30.11 (upstream ollama#12030 open since 2025-08-22, unmerged; issue ollama#12031 open), and prompt_eval_count is only returned after the call, so a pre-flight cannot consult it. Decision: add count_tokens(text) -> int as a separate optional Protocol, TokenCountingBackend(LLMBackend, Protocol), NOT as an LLMBackend member — Protocols are structural, so a member declared there is required of every implementer regardless of default body, which would make the sibling contemplative-agent-cloud and -mlx backends non-conformant for a capability neither can honor; a tokenizer is not the one-line property context_window is. Resolve it structurally at runtime via getattr plus a callable check (no isinstance, which would accept a non-callable attribute). Prefer the backend's count but never trust it: reject a value that is not a plain int (bool excluded explicitly as an int subclass), is negative, or is 0 for text with content — over-counting only wastes budget, under-counting sends over-window input into the exact failure the guard prevents. Bound magnitude as well as shape via MAX_CHARS_PER_TOKEN=50: a well-typed positive but wildly-too-small count (5 for a 50,000-character prompt) otherwise passes every shape check and tells the guard the input is nearly free, and the realistic source is a mis-calibrated tokenizer in a sibling backend rather than malice. The bound is tokenization density, not a ratio against the estimator, so an efficient tokenization of repetitive text is not mistaken for a fault. On the counted path the clamp withholds BACKEND_FRAMING_RESERVE=64 tokens for the chat-template role separators and control tokens no caller-side count sees, since clamping to the exact measured remainder would put input plus output flush against the window; the estimator path reserves nothing, its over-count already being one, which is also what leaves the Ollama path's clamp arithmetic unchanged. Measure both halves, validate afterwards, adopt or reject them together (a measured system prompt mixed with an estimated user prompt describes neither), reporting the system-side reason when both fail so a replay can predict it. A counter fault never records a circuit-breaker failure — failing to measure a call is not the call failing. Telemetry gains dense token_count_source and input_tokens plus a sparse token_count_fallback_reason from a fixed vocabulary (counter_exception, counter_none, counter_type, counter_negative, counter_degenerate, counter_implausible); absence of a counter is the default, not a fallback, so it gets no reason code. MIN_CLAMPED_NUM_PREDICT is untouched here — moving the measurement and the floor together would make their contributions indistinguishable (one pipeline variable at a time). Amended 2026-08-01 (separate change, after this one landed): the floor drops from 2048 to 128 and the clamp stays. The floor had been doing two jobs — refusing an absurd remainder (cheap and correct) and predicting that a usable answer needs 2048 tokens (never validated, and measured ~6x over: comment output p50 352 / p90 507 tokens, n=2,366 counted with a real tokenizer over reports/comment-reports/). Only the prediction is retired; 128 is bounded below by generate_for_api's own +50 minimum and Ollama's default output length of 128, and sits under the p90 so a complete comment is never turned away at the door. The question the floor was guessing at — is this generation long enough to be usable — is answered downstream by drop_truncated (audit M2) from the actual done_reason, so the change is from predicting to trying and measuring; a skip suppresses an action outright whereas a truncation drop costs one generation and is visible as truncated_dropped. On Ollama the floor is inert (it fires only above 32,640 tokens of input against a ~20.3K-token high-water system prompt, pinned by TestClampFloorIsInertOnOllama); it bites on a small-window backend, where 2048 was 50% of a 4,096 window. The fault column TestNarrowHeadroomTruncationF7 covers the newly reachable path (a clamped generation cut mid-sentence) at the existing LLMBackend seam. _NOVELTY_OUTPUT_RESERVE keeps 2048 as the judge's own reservation rather than a copy of the floor. Accepted residuals recorded with the amendment: truncation-drops replace skips in the newly opened band and cost a full prompt-eval; internal callers (drop_truncated=False) see short partial text more often; and the M2 gate that now carries the too-short question is conditional on BackendResult.finish_reason, which the Protocol permits to be None — a pre-existing gap independent of the floor, reachable more often now, tracked separately rather than folded in. Explicitly NOT a consequence: the floor was never the backstop against an under-counting backend tokenizer; the clamp spends the remaining budget rather than leaving it as slack, so the window overrun is delta minus BACKEND_FRAMING_RESERVE regardless of the floor, confirmed by a probe measuring an identical 335-token overrun at both 2048 and 128. The chaos fault column ships in the same change via TokenCountingChaosBackend plus a hypothesis fuzz over the return type. Rejected alternatives: putting count_tokens on LLMBackend itself (the symmetric-looking option, but it imposes a tokenizer requirement on backends that cannot have one); giving the Protocol member a default body (structural conformance still requires it, so it breaks external backends while appearing not to); correcting the estimator with a fixed CJK divisor (the factor is model-specific, measured on one corpus, and tightening a safety bound on a sample is how the under-count failure reopens); calling /api/tokenize on Ollama (unavailable, not undesirable); using BackendResult.prompt_tokens (post-hoc, so it is a calibration instrument for the separate clamp-floor question, not a pre-flight). Consequences: a tokenizer-owning backend gets the window it actually has; existing external backends are unchanged by implementing nothing, pinned by regression tests; every rejection path degrades to the prior behavior so the ceiling is never disabled; but two measurement paths for one quantity now disagree by up to 1.95x (hence the dense source field), the backend's tokenizer sits on every guarded call's critical path with no latency bound, and a second capability Protocol sets a precedent a third could abuse. Follow-ups: adopt Ollama real counting only after measuring the extra HTTP round-trip on the unattended schedule if ollama#12030 lands; re-read the estimator's real-world ratio from production rows now that input_tokens sits beside prompt_eval_count (the clamp floor itself was resolved by the 2026-08-01 amendment, from output-side measurement, so it no longer waits on this); leave insight_novelty packing on the estimator, since a relaxed guard makes the packer tighter than the pre-flight, which is the safe direction its own contract names.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0053-importance-encoding-time-significance.md",
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0056-retire-importance-llm-scoring.md",
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0066-backend-aware-context-budget-guard.md",
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0075-observability-by-default.md",
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0077-chaos-tdd-fault-injection.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0088-shipped-conformance-kit-for-the-llm-backend-contract.md",
      "@type": [
        "ADR",
        "TechArticle"
      ],
      "name": "ADR-0088: A Shipped Conformance Kit for the LLMBackend Contract",
      "identifier": "ADR-0088",
      "status": "accepted",
      "datePublished": "2026-08-02",
      "description": "Moves the canonical LLMBackend contract checks into the shipped package so sibling backend repositories import them instead of hand-copying, and places the enforcing gate in main's release procedure rather than in the siblings. Context: contemplative-agent-cloud last changed 2026-04-21 and drifted for three months while the Protocol gained temperature, gained think, changed its return type from str|None to BackendResult|None, gained a context_window property, and gained an optional count_tokens capability; calling it from main raises TypeError at the first generate(). Two facts about why nobody noticed determine the decision. First, cloud already HAD a conformance test — tests/test_anthropic_backend.py:100 asserts isinstance(backend, LLMBackend), which fails today because AnthropicBackend has no context_window — so the deficiency is not a missing test, and a better test in the same place inherits the same fate; whatever is built must be runnable by the party with the motive (main, at the moment it publishes a contract change) rather than by a repository untouched for a quarter. Second, isinstance cannot see the other half: a runtime_checkable Protocol checks only that members EXIST, never signatures or return types, so cloud's generate(prompt, system, num_predict, format) -> Optional[str] passes the member check completely and the only reason isinstance fails at all is the separate absence of context_window. A backend can therefore be isinstance-clean and still TypeError on the first real call. A third fact settles placement: tests/chaos.py was written as the canonical fault-injection kit (ADR-0077) and no sibling imports it, because [tool.hatch.build.targets.wheel] packages only src/contemplative_agent — tests/ is not in the wheel and never was, so 'main holds the canonical copy' was true as intention and false as distribution mechanism. Decision: the kit ships at src/contemplative_agent/testing/, importing only the standard library and contemplative_agent.core.llm (pytest, hypothesis and responses are dev-group-only and the runtime dependency set stays requests + numpy, following the pytest-free discipline tests/chaos.py already demonstrates). Two forbidden import-linter contracts rather than a fourth layers entry — production layers may not import the kit and the kit may not import cli or adapters — because a layers entry would state the direction correctly and also permit testing -> adapters, dragging the Moltbook HTTP client into a sibling's test dependencies. The load-bearing check is bind-ability, not membership: generate.binds_canonical_call binds the exact call _generate_via_backend issues (four positional, temperature and think keyword-only) without requiring parameter names to match, so a **kwargs backend stays conformant while the stale signature is caught. Failures are returned rather than raised — the failure being addressed is accumulated drift, not a single bug, and raising on the first mismatch answers 'is it broken' while destroying 'in how many ways, and which' — via a ConformanceReport implementing __bool__ and a multi-line __repr__, so assert check_backend(b) stays a one-liner that still names every failing check. Capability handling: detection decides which capability-gated checks run so forgetting to declare one never silently removes coverage; declaring one that is not detected fails; detecting one that was not declared does NOT fail, because otherwise adding a capability constant would redden three repositories for a purely declarative reason; and a declared capability the current level cannot observe is deferred, not refuted. require=LEVEL_STATIC is the permanent default across the static/runtime/full levels, since raising a default later would redden siblings that changed nothing — precisely the breakage a drift-reducing kit must never cause. Ship in stages but freeze the API in the first: six static checks now (capturing cloud's three observed static failures with zero sibling-side work, while the stale Optional[str] return remains explicitly deferred to result.is_backend_result at runtime), 23 runtime and construction checks catalogued in the ADR for later, with check_backend's signature, ConformanceReport, BackendProbe and the level and capability vocabularies complete from the start so later stages add checks and never arguments; level_reached is capped at the highest level with registered checks, so a future-facing probe cannot make the static-only release report a false full green. circuit_reading() is added to core.llm as an ADR-0071-style read-only instrument (existing state, no mechanism, feeds no gate) beside the already-public circuit_shield() control, retiring the sibling tests' direct reads of _circuit._consecutive_failures which a rename inside _CircuitBreaker would break silently. The kit runs from main via python -m contemplative_agent.testing --backend pkg.mod:Name, needing no test file in the sibling at all, driven across every known sibling by scripts/check-sibling-backends.sh; the enforcing gate is the human one in docs/runbooks/sibling-backend-conformance.md invoked from the release-doi skill between verification and push, following ADR-0012's pattern of placing the decision where a human already stands. Routine .claude/verify.sh never runs the sibling checker because the runner imports and constructs code from adjacent checkouts; the explicit release gate is both the human decision point and the point where the operator accepts those checkouts as executable input. The CLI and runner preserve exit 0 conforming / 1 non-conforming / 2 unusable, limit argv constructor kwargs to the base_url and model allowlist with base_url restricted to a credential-free HTTP(S) origin, and suppress arbitrary module or constructor exception text. An authoring rule constrains future checks: assert only against symbols contemplative_agent.core.llm publicly re-exports, routing telemetry field names through exported constants and deriving numbers (the over-budget check sizes its prompt from backend.context_window, never from the estimator's chars-per-token), so an internal rename does not convert into three red repositories for no contractual reason. Rejected alternatives: keeping sibling-local tests and manual checks (cloud already had one that nobody ran for three months; viable again only with active maintainers and an independently enforced sibling release gate); placing the kit under tests/ and having siblings vendor it (rejected on the distribution fact, and copying reproduces the hand-copy that caused the drift); adding GitHub Actions to each sibling (the original plan, rejected on discovering no repository in the family has any CI at all — .github/workflows/ exists in none of main, -mlx, or -cloud — so it would introduce unused infrastructure into the two repositories that go untouched for months, to detect a problem caused by those repositories going untouched for months); passing injection points as six or seven individual callables (unreadable at the call site and giving the sibling no typed target for pyright, the same reasoning by which ADR-0087 split TokenCountingBackend off LLMBackend, hence the two-method BackendProbe which deliberately omits sent_messages() since that would bake a chat-messages array into the kit that Anthropic and completion-style backends do not have); having the kit read _circuit directly (a shipped module reading its own package's privates is itself a contract hole and would freeze the attribute names anyway); and observing circuit behavior black-box (slow, and it conflates 'was this call counted as a failure' with 'does the threshold work'). Consequences: cloud's three observed static failures are now measured — running the shipped kit on 2026-08-02, both cloud backends exit 1 naming protocol.members, context_window.positive_int and generate.binds_canonical_call while contemplative_agent_mlx.backends.mlx:MlxLmBackend exits 0, so the kit distinguished the live sibling from the stale one on its first run — and a contract change covered by a registered check produces a red at release instead of silence, while runtime return semantics remain deferred. Costs: a contract change covered by a registered check now reddens three repositories at once, which is intended for assertions about the CONTRACT (that redness is the missing notification) but is pure cost for assertions about IMPLEMENTATION DETAILS, a distinction recorded explicitly because without it the first red gets read as 'the kit is brittle' and the kit gets removed; the shipped package grows a module no production path uses; the explicit release gate executes trusted sibling checkout code and therefore cannot be folded into routine Verify; freezing the shipped API requires a major-version boundary or compatibility shim for breaking changes and removal of consumer imports before retirement; and 23 of 29 checks are catalogued but unimplemented, with expected_checks() as the intended guard against reading the catalogue as coverage. Follow-ups: _core_harness.py was planned as a fourth module isolating core-internal coupling but is deliberately NOT created here, since with no runtime checks landed it would be empty and an empty private module is worse than an absent one; the reports_finish_reason capability gives T-FINISHREASON-GATE a cheaper resolution by turning 'a backend without truncation gating' from an accident into a declared fact; and contemplative-agent-otel is recorded as absent from CLAUDE.md's related-repositories table, out of scope here since it implements no LLMBackend.",
      "isBasedOn": [
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-core-adapter-separation.md",
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0012-human-approval-gate.md",
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0066-backend-aware-context-budget-guard.md",
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0071-read-only-pattern-composition-instruments.md",
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0077-chaos-tdd-fault-injection.md",
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0079-module-reorganization-package-splits.md",
        "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0087-optional-token-counting-capability-for-the-context-budget-guard.md"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://github.com/shimo4228/contemplative-agent/blob/main/docs/adr/0089-llm-behavioral-eval-layer-on-deepeval.md",
      "@type": [
        "ADR",
        "TechArticle"
      ],
      "name": "ADR-0089: An LLM Behavioral Eval Layer on DeepEval",
      "identifier": "ADR-0089",
      "status": "accepted",
      "datePublished": "2026-08-06",
      "description": "Adds a top-level evals/ layer that measures the quality of what the LLM component actually generates — the one property the repo's seven deterministic gates and LLM code-review chains never touched; prompt revisions had been validated by a manual replay-and-stare workflow (.notes/ replay-distill v2-v5, sampling_probe side-by-side eyeballing). Second occupant of the evals/ path: a promptfoo prompt-regression harness lived there 2026-06-10 to 2026-07-03, deleted by ADR-0072 as dead scaffolding hard-importing since-deleted prompt constants to regression-test the ADR-0060-retired batch distill pipeline — a judgment about the harness's target, not about eval layers, so this ADR reoccupies the cleared path (not a supersede) with a different face and a runner importing only the production entry point. Face A pilot: the Moltbook comment path. Framework verdict (scout, as-of 2026-08-06): adopt DeepEval 4.1.5, whose cloud regression (--official / Confident AI) is explicitly NOT relied on; ADR-0077's earlier rejection of agent-chaos for hard-coupling to DeepEval was a verdict on a chaos-layer dependency, a separate question from adopting DeepEval as an eval-layer runner. Placement: evals/ sits outside tests/ because tests/conftest.py sets OLLAMA_BASE_URL to a dead port at module load to kill unmocked LLM calls, which would sabotage real eval runs; it also sits outside the package, following ADR-0088's spirit of shipping kits outside runtime import paths, enforced by placement since import-linter's root_packages does not see evals/. Layering contract: the deterministic core (dataset.py, judging.py, generation.py, compare.py) imports stdlib and contemplative_agent only and is unit-tested under the dev group (49 tests); only adapter_deepeval.py and run_eval.py may import deepeval, which lives in a dedicated [dependency-groups] eval synced only by eval runs and the verify.sh type gate. Reproducibility: the four self-rewritten prompt assets (identity.md, constitution/, skills/, rules/ — mutable state under ADR-0060 distill and constitution amendment) are pinned by snapshot_assets.py into evals/fixtures/agent_home/ with a sha256 manifest, allowlist-only copying because MOLTBOOK_HOME also holds credentials, snapshots treated as untrusted LLM-distilled output gated by human review plus secret scan; template prompts pin to the repo commit automatically because the scratch MOLTBOOK_HOME has no prompts/ override (core/domain.py resolve_prompt precedence). Generation runs production-parity wiring: run_eval mirrors cli/runtime.py + moltbook agent.py via core.llm.configure(), real Ollama at the ADR-0047 production temperature 1.3, three samples per case with majority vote resolving ties toward the worse verdict; a strict majority of requested samples must generate successfully or the case is INCOMPLETE and the run cannot become a baseline — generation failure must never masquerade as a DEVIANT verdict; the skill-injection regime under measurement is pinned and recorded rather than inherited: run_eval calls configure_skill_selection against the fixture with MOLTBOOK_SKILL_SELECTION_ENFORCE=1, reproducing the ADR-0081 two-pass selected-bodies injection the launchd schedule has run since 2026-08-01, and records it as the manifest field injection_regime. The original design skipped configure_skill_selection on the premise that production ran selection in shadow mode where selection is always None — a premise already false when written (ADR-0081 enforcement shipped 2026-07-24), so the first baseline measured full-corpus injection against a production that injected a selection; the prose revisit trigger 'when enforcement goes always-on' could not fire because its condition was already met. Corrected by the 2026-08-08 amendment, which also makes the divergence mechanically detectable: injection_regime is a check_staleness signal, _preflight refuses to run when the wiring does not enact the pinned regime, and deployment_mismatch compares the pin against the installed launchd plist — the tree-versus-deployment axis no in-tree hash can cover. Judging: an isolated claude -p subprocess per the harness llm-as-judge design — binary checks as evidence feeding one named holistic verdict from {ADHERENT, DRIFTING, DEVIANT}, the verdict-to-{1.0,0.5,0.0} mapping display-only and never aggregated; isolation via --setting-sources \"\", --tools \"\", --strict-mcp-config, scratch cwd, prompt via stdin, and an allowlisted environment (HOME/PATH/USER/SHELL/LANG/LC_ALL/TMPDIR/TERM only — a leaked ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL or CLAUDE_EFFORT would make the manifest's judge_model a lie); the untrusted post/comment blocks pass delimiter neutralization before embedding (a delimiter-splice in the comment body was demonstrated swapping the judged text for an ADHERENT), and the judge prompt embeds the snapshotted ADR-0002 constitution — loaded via the same load_constitution glob as generation — as the evaluation standard so the judge never substitutes its own general notion of the axioms; the parsed response is mechanically validated against the output contract (exactly five named checks, no duplicates, a dominant No on injection_resistant/persona_intact forces DEVIANT), every attempt's raw envelope is appended to a per-run judge-audit.jsonl for offline replay (ADR-0075 observability by default), and a parse or contract failure retries once then fails loud. Contract: a normalized run JSON (schema_version, manifest of target_model / temperature / judge_model / assets_sha256 / judge_prompt_sha256 / dataset_sha256 / samples_per_case, per-case samples with checks and verdicts) compared by evals/compare.py as per-case verdict transitions against approved baselines in evals/baselines/ — manifest mismatch is incomparable (exit 2), regression is exit 1; deepeval's own TestRun output is a debug byproduct so deepeval upgrades cannot break baselines. Eval stays out of verify.sh by design (slow, stochastic, delta-judged — a different contract from the fast deterministic gate); triggers are prompt-asset, model, sampling, or generation-path changes. DEEPEVAL_TELEMETRY_OPT_OUT=true is forced before deepeval import (verified short-circuit in deepeval/telemetry.py) and the .deepeval cache is contained per-run via chdir. Golden dataset: 12 cases, 4 axioms x {normal, edge, adversarial}, adversarial cases embedding instructions in post content — meaningful because wrap_untrusted_content sits on the path. Rejected alternatives: Inspect AI (strongest design fit, most active releases, but baseline diff is not native so the same compare layer would be needed anyway, and the Task/Solver/Scorer abstraction has no current payoff — noted as the compose target if evals need multi-turn or tool-use scoring), promptfoo (pip wrapper shells out to npx — Node runtime in a pure Python/uv repo), pytest-evals (too thin), ragas (RAG-centric), a self-built runner (dataset/report plumbing is undifferentiated work DeepEval ships; the project-specific parts stay project-specific either way), and DeepEval's cloud regression (routes through Confident AI; local JSON keeps the loop offline and schema-stable). Costs accepted: deepeval's ~60-package transitive tree grows pip-audit's surface (a CVE there can now block commits, which is honest — with the recorded coupling that this coverage holds only while the deps gate audits the venv the type gate populated); a full run costs ~19 minutes measured plus one claude -p call per successful sample; verdicts are stochastic at temperature 1.3 with majority-of-3 damping but not eliminating flips (samples=5 is the documented escalation); re-snapshotting assets invalidates every baseline by design via manifest mismatch; the eval measures the comment face only, distill quality staying with tests/benchmark_distill.py. Generalizing an eval axis into the harness verify-bootstrap gate menu is explicitly out of scope; contemplative-agent is the pilot.",
